    "narrative_title": "Geopolitical energy shock and scramble for supply",
    "narrative_summary": "The dominant narrative is a geopolitically-driven global energy supply shock: wars, sanctions, shipping disruptions and refinery outages are tightening oil, diesel and gas flows, driving record fuel prices and inflationary pressure. The system is open — exogenous shocks (conflict, sanctions, weather) and endogenous responses (strategic oil deals, stockpiling, policy measures) change stocks (inventories), flows (exports, shipments, refining) and feedbacks (prices → political pressure → policy → market behaviour). Consequences cascade into Europe’s winter gas security, markets and fiscal/political responses, prompting state and corporate actions to secure or redistribute supply.",
    "scope": "Crude oil, refined products (diesel, gasoline), natural gas, storage/inventories, refineries and logistics (shipping routes, tanker flows, chokepoints like Hormuz), major producing and consuming states, energy firms and traders, commodity markets and futures, related policy instruments (sanctions, strategic deals, EU energy policy), and near-term demand shocks (seasonal winter demand, El Niño).",
    "super_system": "Global geopolitical-economy and energy-security nexus",
    "adjacent_subsystems": [
      "European domestic politics and migration policy",
      "Global commodity and financial markets (trading, insurers)",
      "Energy transition, climate policy and renewable investment",
      "Regional security dynamics in the Middle East and Latin America"
    ],
    "evidence_headlines": [
      "US diesel prices hit record high as conflicts intensify supply crunch",
